*.aidocs linguist-language=Markdown
*.sh text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf
install.sh text eol=lf

# PYTHON SOURCES ARE LF EVERYWHERE (2026-07-25; rationale corrected 2026-07-26).
#
# CORRECTION — READ THIS BEFORE TRUSTING THE ORIGINAL CLAIM. The commit that
# added these rules (ea3383c3) asserted that CRLF caused the advertised TOOL
# SURFACE to differ between platforms, via griffelib failing to match "Args:\r".
# THAT WAS WRONG and is disproven: .py is LF everywhere now and Windows still
# emitted the same surface it did before. The real cause was never line endings —
# every tool's description is overridden at registration from gate_message_strings
# in ~/.aidocs/empire.sqlite3, and when that machine-local database is absent the
# raw source docstring stands instead (#533, fixed in dc7369483).
#
# THESE RULES STAY, on their own merits rather than the retracted one:
#   * Reproducibility. Everything listed below is parsed as TEXT by tooling
#     (ruff, basedpyright, semgrep, vulture, the AST fingerprint registry, the
#     golden pins). A file whose bytes differ per checkout is a file whose
#     tool verdicts can differ per checkout, and a BLOCKING gate must not depend
#     on who cloned the repo.
#   * The deploy ships LF regardless: the ship stage is `git archive HEAD`, so
#     dev matching that is one less axis of divergence.
#   * It matches the pre-existing *.sh / *.bash rules rather than leaving Python
#     as the odd exception.
#
# Kept as a worked example of a real trap: six hypotheses were wrong before #533
# was found (encoding, mcp SDK version, griffelib version, the 3.12->3.13
# docstring dedent gh-81283, editable-install source resolution, and CRLF). Each
# looked plausible; each was already identical across both environments.
*.py text eol=lf
*.pyi text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
